Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>Appendix D - A Style Guide checklist</H1>
<HR>
Acorn's <I>RISC OS 3 Style Guide</I> (1993) describes how a RISC OS application should look and behave, so
that everything a user learns in one program works in the next. This appendix is a checklist of its rules
for the kind of application this book builds, with the chapter where !Contacts or !Organiser does each
thing. Go through it for your own applications.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>The icon bar</H2>
<UL>
<LI>One icon on the icon bar. Select on it opens a new document (or the main window) (7).<P>
<LI>Menu on it gives at least <I>Info</I> and <I>Quit</I>, and <I>Choices...</I> if there are choices
(7, 9).<P>
<LI>Files dragged onto it are loaded (7, 8).<P>
<LI>Its interactive help says what clicking does (9).<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Documents and files</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Documents have a file type of their own, with a name and an icon, set up in <CODE>!Boot</CODE> (7).<P>
<LI>Double-clicking a document loads it; if the program is already running, that copy loads it (7).<P>
<LI>A document that is already open is brought to the front, not loaded twice (7).<P>
<LI>The document's full name is in its window's title, followed by <CODE>*</CODE> as soon as it has unsaved
changes; the <CODE>*</CODE> goes when it is saved (7).<P>
<LI>Other programs' formats (CSV, vCard, iCalendar) are loaded when dropped on you, not claimed as your own
file types, and exported with Save boxes of their own (8, 16).<P>
<LI>The file format is written down, and carries a version number (7; appendix E).<P>
<LI><I>Info</I> about the document shows its name, whether it's changed and how much it holds (9).<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Saving</H2>
<UL>
<LI>F3 opens the Save box; the file icon can be dragged to a directory display or into another program
(7, 8).<P>
<LI>A name with a full path can be typed instead, and OK (or Return) saves (7).<P>
<LI>Saving clears the "modified" mark and updates the title (7).<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Closing and quitting</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Closing a changed document asks: Discard, Cancel or Save (7).<P>
<LI>Quitting, or the desktop shutting down (PreQuit), asks about unsaved changes before anything is lost
(9).<P>
<LI>Nothing is kept in memory after its window closes without the user knowing (7).<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Menus</H2>
<UL>
<LI>The window's menu is built each time it opens, from the current state: items that can't be used are
shaded, options that are on are ticked (4).<P>
<LI>Item names start with a capital and go on in small letters: <I>Select all</I>, not <I>Select All</I>.
(The book's menus follow this throughout.)<P>
<LI>An item that opens a dialogue box that stays until closed ends with <I>...</I> (9).<P>
<LI>Shortcut keys are shown at the right of their items (4).<P>
<LI>Menu over an unselected item selects it just for the menu (2).<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Dialogue boxes</H2>
<UL>
<LI>The default action is the button at the bottom right, with the thicker border; Return does it, Escape
cancels, and Cancel is to its left (3).<P>
<LI>Buttons are named with verbs: <I>Save</I>, <I>Delete</I>, not <I>Yes</I> and <I>No</I> (3, 4).<P>
<LI>Tab, Shift-Tab and the arrow keys move between fields; Return moves to the next field until the last
(3).<P>
<LI>Nothing changes until the action button is pressed; the dialogue box edits a copy (3).<P>
<LI>A problem is reported, and the caret put in the field that needs changing (3).<P>
<LI>A question that could lose work (Delete) asks first, if the user wants it to (4, 9).<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Selection</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Select chooses one item; Adjust adds or removes one; double-click opens (2).<P>
<LI>Ctrl-A selects everything; Ctrl-Z clears the selection (4).<P>
<LI>What is selected is shown in reverse (2).<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Toolbars and toolboxes</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Choices made again and again go in a toolbar or toolbox: a pane attached to the document's window, like
Draw's (5, 11).<P>
<LI>Its buttons are 3D, repeat things in the menus, and are shaded when they can't be used (5).<P>
<LI>Every button has interactive help (9).<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>The keyboard</H2>
<UL>
<LI>A window gets the input focus when it's clicked in or opened; keys it doesn't use are passed on, so F12
and the other desktop keys always work (2).<P>
<LI>The standard keys that !Contacts and !Organiser have, with the chapter that adds each:
<PRE>
F3  save (7)           F4  find (6)         F6  sort (6)
F8  undo (9)           F9  redo (9)         Print  print (16)
Insert  new (4)        Delete  delete (4)   ^A  select all (4)
^Z  clear selection (4)                     ^D  the date (14)
</PRE>
The Style Guide has more that these programs don't use: F1 for help, F5 to go to a place, ^F2 to close
the window. (F8, F9 and ^A are !Contacts'; the organiser has no undo.)<P>
<LI>Alt is never used for shortcuts.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Colour</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Colour is used only inside the data area: window frames, menus and dialogue boxes keep the desktop's
own colours (10).<P>
<LI>Colour is never the only difference: overdue to-dos have a symbol as well, planner categories a pattern
as well as a colour (13, 15).<P>
<LI>Not too many colours, and the user can change them (16).<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S11></A>
<H2>Choices</H2>
<UL>
<LI><I>Choices...</I> on the icon bar menu opens a box with Default, Cancel, Set (use them now) and Save
(use them and keep them) (9).<P>
<LI>Choices are saved in <CODE>Choices:</CODE>, not in the application's directory (9).<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S12></A>
<H2>Help</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Every window, icon, menu item and the icon bar icon has interactive help (9).<P>
<LI>The application directory has a <CODE>!Help</CODE> file (9).<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S13></A>
<H2>Errors</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Messages are plain and say what to do: no numbers, no "fatal", at most one short apology. (The book's
programs keep to this; no chapter is about it.)<P>
<LI>Error boxes are centred on the screen; boxes opened from a menu, near the pointer. The book's
dialogue boxes don't quite follow this: <CODE>Form</CODE> opens them in the middle of the screen, a third
of the way down. Opening them near the pointer instead is a good exercise.<P>
</UL>

